
--1. Basics of Digital Systems
Digital systems process information using binary values, 0 and 1. They use logic gates,
memory, and a clock. Examples are computers, phones, and digital controllers.
--2. Digital systems based on computer engineering complexes
These systems combine hardware and software. The hardware includes a processor,
memory, input/output devices, and buses. Software controls the hardware and processes
data.
--3. Digital system and its fundamental principles of operation
A digital system receives input data, processes it with logic or a program, stores data if
needed, and produces an output. It represents information with discrete values, usually 0
and 1.
--4. Signal reconstruction from discrete samples
Signal reconstruction means creating a continuous signal from its samples. If the signal is
sampled fast enough, a reconstruction filter can recover it accurately.
--5. Data conversion and quantization
Data conversion changes data from one form to another, such as analog to digital.
Quantization assigns each sample to the nearest value from a limited set of levels.
--6. Sample-and-hold circuits
A sample-and-hold circuit takes the input voltage at a certain time and holds it constant
for a short time. It helps an ADC measure a stable signal.
