--7. Digital-to-analog conversion
A DAC converts a digital number into an analog voltage or current. The output changes
according to the digital input. It is used in audio and control systems.
--8. Analog-to-digital conversion
An ADC converts an analog signal into digital numbers. The main steps are sampling,
quantization, and binary encoding.
--9. Discretization of continuous-time processes
Discretization represents a continuous-time signal by taking samples at regular time
intervals. The sampling period is T = 1/fs.
--10. Reconstruction of continuous functions
A continuous function can be reconstructed from samples when the sampling theorem is
satisfied. An ideal low-pass filter can recover a band-limited signal.
--11. Fourier transform
The Fourier transform shows the frequency components of a signal. It changes a signal
from the time domain to the frequency domain.
--12. Modified Z-transform
The modified Z-transform is used for sampled-data systems with sampling or delay
effects. It helps analyze digital control systems and their responses.
--13. Analog model of the extrapolator
An extrapolator estimates the signal between samples using past samples. A zero-order
hold keeps each sample constant until the next sample; a first-order hold uses a straight
line.
--14. Stable and unstable systems
A stable system gives a bounded output for every bounded input. An unstable system
may give an output that grows without limit. For a discrete-time system, poles must be
inside the unit circle for BIBO stability in common rational systems.
--15. Design of digital filters and BIBO stability
A digital filter removes or changes selected frequency components. BIBO stability means
every bounded input gives a bounded output. For a causal IIR filter, all poles must be
inside the unit circle.
--16. Routh–Hurwitz criterion for stability
The Routh–Hurwitz criterion checks the stability of a continuous-time system from its
characteristic polynomial. The system is stable if all entries in the first column of the
Routh table have the same sign and no special-case issue occurs.
--17. Sampling, quantization, and convolution
Sampling takes values at separate times. Quantization rounds each sample to one of a
finite number of levels. Convolution combines an input signal with a system's impulse
response to find the output.
--18. Nyquist criterion for stability
The Nyquist criterion uses the open-loop frequency response to determine closed-loop
stability. It studies how the Nyquist plot surrounds the point -1 + j0, together with the
number of open-loop unstable poles.
--19. Digital controller and D/A conversion
A digital controller reads sampled data, calculates a control value, and sends it to the
plant. A DAC converts the digital control value into an analog signal for the physical
system.
--20. IIR realization: direct, parallel, and cascaded
Direct form implements the transfer function using delays, multipliers, and adders.
Parallel form splits it into simpler sections added together. Cascaded form connects
sections one after another.
--21. Transfer functions
A transfer function is the ratio of the output Z-transform to the input Z-transform when
initial conditions are zero. It describes the input-output behavior of a system.
--22. Modulation and demodulation
Modulation changes a carrier signal according to the message signal. Demodulation
recovers the message from the modulated signal. These processes are used in
communication systems.
--23. Coefficient quantization effects in digital filters
Coefficient quantization stores filter coefficients with limited precision. It can change the
frequency response, move poles, increase error, and sometimes make a filter unstable.
--24. Digital Signal Processing System
A DSP system converts real-world signals into digital data, processes them, and may
convert them back to analog form. Common blocks are an ADC, processor, memory, and
DAC.
--25. Quadrature modulation and deviation
Quadrature modulation uses two carrier signals that are 90 degrees apart. The two signals
can carry separate data at the same time. In FM, frequency deviation is the maximum
change from the carrier frequency.
--26. Spectral characteristics
Spectral characteristics show how signal power or amplitude is distributed over
frequency. They help identify bandwidth, noise, and important frequency components.
--27. Modulation and demodulation
Modulation puts information onto a carrier for transmission. Demodulation extracts the
original information at the receiver.
--28. Discrete-time systems
A discrete-time system works with samples defined at separate time points. Its input and
output are sequences, such as x[n] and y[n].
--29. IIR filter design using the bilinear Z-transform
The bilinear transform maps an analog filter into a digital filter using z = (1 + sT/2)/(1 
sT/2). It maps the stable left half-plane inside the unit circle, but it causes frequency
warping.
--30. FIR and IIR filters
An FIR filter has a finite impulse response and usually no feedback. An IIR filter has an
infinite impulse response and usually uses feedback. FIR filters can have exact linear
phase.
--31. Recursive smoother
A recursive smoother reduces rapid changes and noise by using the current input and a
previous output. A simple form is y[n] = a x[n] + (1-a)y[n-1], where 0<a<1.
--32. Group delay, linear phase, all-pass, minimum phase
Group delay is the delay of signal components at different frequencies. A linear-phase
filter delays all frequencies equally. An all-pass filter changes phase but keeps magnitude.
A minimum-phase filter has its zeros inside the unit circle and the smallest delay for its
magnitude response.
--33. Analog-to-digital conversion
ADC changes a continuous analog signal into digital values. It samples the signal,
quantizes the samples, and encodes them as binary numbers.
--34. Fundamental definition of system stability
A system is stable if a bounded input produces a bounded output. This is called BIBO
stability. In an unstable system, the output may grow without limit.
--35. Sampling and quantization
Sampling measures a signal at regular time intervals. Quantization maps each measured
value to one of a finite set of levels. Both are important in digital conversion.
--36. Aliasing and anti-aliasing
Aliasing happens when the sampling frequency is too low, so high frequencies appear as
lower frequencies. An anti-aliasing low-pass filter removes high frequencies before
sampling.
--37. Nyquist–Shannon sampling theorem
The theorem says that the sampling frequency must be greater than twice the highest
signal frequency for accurate reconstruction. Thus, fs>2fmax in practical use.
--38. Correlation analysis
Correlation measures how similar two signals are and how one signal changes with
another. Autocorrelation compares a signal with a delayed version of itself; cross
correlation compares two signals.
--39. Equalization filters
An equalization filter compensates for unwanted changes caused by a channel or system.
It can make the output closer to the original signal.
--40. Frequency response and transfer function
The transfer function describes a system in the transform domain. The frequency
response is found by evaluating it on the unit circle, usually at z = exp(jω). It shows gain
and phase versus frequency.
--41. Quantization error
Quantization error is the difference between the original sample and its quantized value.
For rounding quantization, the error is often between -Δ/2 and +Δ/2, where Δ is the step
size.
--42. All-pass filters
An all-pass filter has a constant magnitude response at all frequencies, but it changes
phase. It is used to adjust phase or group delay.
--43. Fourier transform and digital Fourier transform
The Fourier transform represents a signal by its frequency components. The discrete
Fourier transform (DFT) does this for a finite sequence of samples and is often calculated
with the FFT algorithm.
--44. First-order notch filter
A notch filter removes a narrow range of frequencies. A first-order notch filter is a simple
filter used to reduce an unwanted frequency, although a second-order design is more
common for a sharp notch.
--45. Second-order unity-gain resonator
A second-order resonator gives a strong response near a selected frequency. Unity gain
means the gain is 1 at the specified reference or center frequency, according to the
design.
--46. State space
State-space modeling describes a system using state variables. A common form is x[k+1]
= Ax[k] + Bu[k] and y[k] = Cx[k] + Du[k].
--47. Transformation of random signals in discrete-time systems
A discrete-time system changes the statistical properties of a random input signal. For a
linear time-invariant system, the output mean and power spectrum depend on the input
and the system response.
--48. High-pass filters
A high-pass filter passes high frequencies and reduces low frequencies. It is used to
remove slow changes or low-frequency noise.
--49. Low-pass filters
A low-pass filter passes low frequencies and reduces high frequencies. It is used to
smooth signals and remove high-frequency noise.
--50. Band-pass filters
A band-pass filter passes frequencies within a selected band and reduces frequencies
below and above that band. It is used to select a useful frequency range.
--51. Relationship between sampling frequency and maximum
signal frequency
According to the sampling theorem, the sampling frequency must be at least twice the
maximum frequency: fs ≥ 2fmax. In practice, fs is chosen higher than 2fmax to allow a
safety margin and filter transition band.
--52. Nyquist frequency, aliasing, and reconstruction
The Nyquist frequency is half the sampling frequency: fN = fs/2. Aliasing occurs when the
signal has frequencies above this limit. Reconstruction uses a suitable filter to recover
the original band-limited signal.
--53. Signal up to 8 kHz; sampling at 12 kHz
The highest frequency is 8 kHz, so the theoretical minimum sampling frequency is 16 kHz.
Sampling at 12 kHz is too low because its Nyquist frequency is 6 kHz. Aliasing may occur,
so accurate reconstruction is not possible.
--54. Fundamental principles of digital system analysis
Digital system analysis studies input, output, and internal behavior. Important tools
include difference equations, the Z-transform, transfer functions, poles, zeros, frequency
response, and stability tests.
--55. Role of the Z-transform
The Z-transform changes a discrete-time sequence into a function of z. It makes
difference equations easier to solve and helps find transfer functions, poles, zeros, and
stability.
--56. Linearity, time invariance, causality, and stability
Linearity means the system obeys superposition. Time invariance means a time shift in
the input causes the same shift in the output. Causality means the output depends only
on present and past inputs. Stability means bounded input gives bounded output.
--57. Effect of poles and zeros
Poles strongly affect stability and the natural response. Zeros reduce or cancel the
response at some frequencies. Pole locations affect resonance and decay, while zeros
shape the frequency response.
--58. Transfer function of a discrete-time system
The transfer function is H(z) = Y(z)/X(z), assuming zero initial conditions. It describes how
the system changes the input sequence into the output sequence.
--59. Obtaining transfer function from a difference equation
Take the Z-transform of both sides of the difference equation and assume zero initial
conditions. Then collect the Y(z) terms and X(z) terms, and divide to get H(z) = Y(z)/X(z).
--60. Relationship between transfer function, poles, zeros, stability,
and frequency response
The transfer function contains poles and zeros. Poles determine stability and natural
response; zeros shape the response. The frequency response is H(z) evaluated on the
unit circle. For a causal rational digital system, poles inside the unit circle indicate stability.
